<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucas Watzenrode</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1447">1447</a>–<a href="/years/1512">1512</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Lucas Watzenrode the Younger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucas Watzenrode was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Warmia from 1489 until his death, a powerful figure in Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> who defended his see's autonomy against both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. He was the uncle and patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, whom he brought into the cathedral chapter at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frombork</a>, where Copernicus later developed his heliocentric theory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucas-watzenrode.jpg" alt="Lucas Watzenrode" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C5%81ukasz_Watzenrode.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
